# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in the working tree.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites them to CRLF on
# checkout. The blobs in the repository are LF and look fine in review, but the
# checked-out file is not runnable: bash reads the trailing \r as part of each
# command, so `scripts/staged_test.sh preflight` dies at line 33 with
# `$'\r': command not found` before it does anything. scripts/setup.sh — the
# documented way to bring up and seed the stack — fails the same way.
#
# eol=lf overrides autocrlf for these paths, so a Windows clone gets a file it
# can actually run under WSL2, Git Bash, or inside a container.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by tools that do not tolerate CRLF, and they
# are copied into Linux images as-is.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
